codemap 9
tour gui-authoring
kind layer
author ai
group gui
note How a human builds a tour in the GUI. The tour being read is the one steps go into; Nav's target is the step they hang under (None = top level). Selecting a step sets the target, moving the selection off the tour keeps it, and the 'top level' button in the target strip clears it, so a reader can collect several symbols under one step. Every add goes through App::add_step. The target strip's add-offer@<view> button, also the palette's 'add step', adds the selected Source lines or the focused symbol. The other adds are the Source view's 'add step' (selected lines), '+ step' on Symbols and References rows (Frame::row_with_action), 'add step' under the References heading, and '+ step' on an off-tour graph node, which hangs under the step it was revealed from as a callee. Adding moves neither the selection nor the target. Rearranging is a drag in the Tours steps list: a press grabs a row (StepGrab), travel past GRAB_REACH makes it a drag, and the release drops it onto a row's zone - top quarter before, bottom quarter after, the middle under - through Map::place; steps_moved then carries every slot-keyed thing (views, history, selection, target) to the steps' new slots. The Tours tab's '+ new tour' button opens the build-tour wizard (feature-build-tour), and the 'promote' button (promote at depth 1) makes a tour from the focused symbol. The feature-add-step, feature-choose-target, feature-move-step, feature-build-tour and feature-promote-symbol tours follow each gesture. The wizard sets a new tour's kind, group and note; after that, the edit-tour page (the pencil on the tour header, feature-edit-tour) renames it, sets its kind, group, tour note and step notes, removes steps and adds calls in one apply; links are written through the Console.

